A fuller repro for the finding:

```ets
function takeAll(...items: string[]): void {
  for (let item of items) {
    console.println(item)
  }
}

function main(): void {
  const s: string = "abc"

  // Existing language behavior/spec: string is iterable.
  for (let ch of s) {
    console.println(ch)
  }

  // Spec-valid: spread expression accepts any iterable type.
  // Expected: compile, producing ["a", "b", "c"].
  let chars: string[] = [...s]

  // Spec-valid: spread into rest parameter from iterable string.
  // Expected: compile, equivalent to takeAll("a", "b", "c").
  takeAll(...s)

  // Also spec-valid: a union of iterable types is iterable.
  let u: string | string[] = s
  let chars2: string[] = [...u]
}
```

Why this should compile: `plugins/ets/doc/spec/17_experimental.rst` says string types are iterable, and `7_expressions.rst` says spread expressions accept iterable or tuple expressions.

What the last commit does instead: `SpreadElementTypeCollector::Collect` only accepts tuple, array, union whose constituents all pass, or object types with `$_iterator`; `ETSStringType` falls through to invalid. So `...s` and unions containing `string` are rejected despite being spec-valid.

